# The red, on the unfixed source

The tree is `23deb8c3` with `681fbd3f`'s two case files alone — `tools/loop/tests/loop-cases.sh` (the new case, its
row in `LOOP_SHELL_CASES` and its dispatch arm) and `tools/loop/tests/loop-mutations.sh` (the two mutations that follow
the moved paragraphs) — checked out over it (`git checkout 681fbd3f -- tools/loop/tests/loop-cases.sh
tools/loop/tests/loop-mutations.sh`). Every other file is the base's, so `loop.conf` records no
`LOOP_WORKER_PROMPT_MAX_BYTES`, `run.sh` renders no reference and `worker.md` still carries the three blocks whole.

## 1. The base as it stands: the size every turn re-sends has no bound

$ bash tools/loop/tests/loop-selftest.sh --case 'run#theWorkerPromptFitsItsBudget'
run#theWorkerPromptFitsItsBudget|   loop.conf records no LOOP_WORKER_PROMPT_MAX_BYTES — the size every turn of an item re-sends has no bound to be read against
--- run#theWorkerPromptFitsItsBudget[0] (exit 1)
  loop.conf records no LOOP_WORKER_PROMPT_MAX_BYTES — the size every turn of an item re-sends has no bound to be read against
FAIL run#theWorkerPromptFitsItsBudget
loop-selftest: 1 of 1 cases failed
exit 1

The case's first assertion is the one that fires, and it is the root cause stated as a red: the bound is read out of
`loop.conf` and there is no key to read, so the size that is charged at every turn of every item is measured against
nothing. The assertions that follow it — the measurement, the pointer, the reference's three blocks, the over-bound
line — are unreachable on this tree.

## 2. The bound alone, and the prompt it would bound: 33 726 B against 29 000 B

With the key added to the base's `loop.conf` and nothing else changed — `LOOP_WORKER_PROMPT_MAX_BYTES=29000` written
beside `LOOP_ITEM_CALLS_PER_TURN_MIN`, `worker.md` and `run.sh` still the base's — the same case runs and reaches the
measurement it exists for:

